{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794231157,"nonce":"YmQQCV5ZjtBfqTodxWP6JGuPfVQ=","message":"Why 0.6.1 is an immediate upgrade from 0.6.0\n\nFound building the russl shader toy (a code editor, tabs, resizable panels and a cn sidebar in one window). Each bug below is in the published 0.6.0, checked against the v0.6.0 tag. Each has its own issue with the reproducer and a regression test that fails without the fix. Fixes are in the working tree, not yet committed.\n\nApps freeze or stop responding\n\n- f289467f5e6d46b1209f9ba9da92975867f9f141ff3d7421c9430e7954e026d7: A code editor's on_change deadlocks if it reads the editor. 0.6.0 calls on_change while holding the editor's lock, so a handler that reads the text, sets diagnostics or recompiles hangs the input thread on the first keystroke. Any editor that reacts to its own content hits it.\n- 18b0aae342ffbcf1301296e75a259181f155671d6be0674d2479af7a417e4790: Widgets go dead after the first list or branch change. A `DivRef::new()` bound to an element with no id gives it one during the build. That renames the stable ids of every descendant at the next region change, and their click handlers are dropped. The render loop, signals and bindings keep running, so the app looks alive while nothing under the element responds. cn's resizable fix in this release avoids it (each panel gets its id at build time). The general hazard for app code that binds a DivRef stays open on that issue.\n\nLayout that does not work\n\n- 0d83ed1057d4e0efa8ec5e9f0e021e94d6ad1077efb7bfd0226979966762c120: Tabs grow to their content, so a code editor or scroll area inside a tab never scrolls.\n- 1fe250247f951f7ce06ed4e0aa46153ddf5de8ff9e3b4fd289f69ea1613ccaea: Dragging a resizable handle beside a flexible panel that has a minimum throws the fixed panel to the wrong size (a sidebar dropped from 341 px to about 66 px on a 50 px drag).\n- 8754f497e225aaf7edb905f5490b5e988c0719c0217ebec9063aed4e0355c986: A flexible resizable panel takes its content's width, so wide content (long code lines) squeezes the fixed panels beside it.\n\nPerformance\n\n- eaabb0202fcef676547b3de7e09ff8e37477b98c6cc11145065b330d8ab57384: Every `class_when` / `attr_when` class matched the whole stylesheet against its element's entire subtree on every full build. With cn's stylesheet loaded, a full build of the toy went from about 35 ms to about 15 ms with the fix (release, headless).\n- 4f4867510200f8f809e64747ce6d452c0a3918a691ef7e4c6abe884b4760f155: Still open. A window resize rebuilds the whole tree on every resize event, so a live resize runs at the full-build cost.\n\nApp guidance to ship with the notes\n\nSignals an app keeps for its whole life must not be created inside the first build (for example lazily from a `OnceLock` first touched in the build closure). The build owns what it creates, and the rebuild a window resize triggers disposes it: the UI then shows empty state. Create such state with `blinc_core::owner::Owner::detached`, or before `run`.\n\nFound but never shipped\n\nA regression in this cycle's resizable drag fix (panels bound to a DivRef without a build-time id) caused the dead-widget symptom above inside every resizable panel. It was caught before commit, and `a_panel_keeps_its_handlers_when_rows_change_elsewhere` guards it.","files":null}]}